virglrenderer: Venus charges recordings and objects for the Mac memory they take

What: each Venus context has a host memory account (OMACVM_VENUS_HOST_MB, else a quarter
of the apps' share of the GPU memory budget, 256 MB to 4 GB), and the same bytes go to
the shared budget. Charged: every object at a fixed estimate per type (event, query pool,
pipeline, pipeline cache 20 KB; command pool 160 KB; command buffer 40 KB; descriptor
pool 12 KB plus a byte per set; shader module, device memory, view, framebuffer, render
pass 4 KB; image 2 KB; buffer, fence, semaphore, sampler, set 512 B; 1 KB the rest;
a query pool 16 B more per query value, a shader module 2 B more per byte of its code
(both drivers keep a copy, vkr its scan: real modules of 17-68 KB took 1.56-1.78 B per
code byte on the mini's M4, 107-122 KB a module at 68 KB of code; the 4 KB covers small
ones: 2644 B of code took 2.33-2.42 B per byte, 6.2-6.4 KB), a pipeline cache 1 B more
per byte of its initial data (a cache made from a driver's own data reports as much
data again: 2597 B on KosmicKrisp, 6570 B on MoltenVK)), and every recording: 512 B per
command, an update's data x 1.5, a Metal pass or a draw that splits one
(virgl-venus-encoder-cap.patch) 184 KB (a split draw measured up to 112941 B), on KosmicKrisp
3 KB per fill and attachment clear and 40 KB per blit or resolve region. A command
buffer takes its context's account 1 MB at a time and keeps the most a recording of it
took (a reset gives nothing back); freed, that goes to its pool, whose next recordings
use it first, and back when the pool goes. Past the account or the budget the call is
refused and the app's context ends (a lost device: the guest made the object or recorded
without waiting); a budget refusal counts the loss as "guard". Everything comes back
where vkr frees the object: after the driver's destroy, a zombie's at its last holder,
and in the context's teardown, which logs the peak. The reported heaps are at most the
apps' share (vkGetPhysicalDeviceMemoryProperties and ...2), maxMemoryAllocationSize
and maxBufferSize at most the heap; VK_EXT_memory_budget reports this context's device
memory as heapUsage and that plus what the share has left as heapBudget (a page at
least, at most the heap). The decoder frees a temp buffer over 16 MB after each stream.
Why: one guest program made QEMU hold Mac memory far past what vkr counted, uncharged and
without a limit: 2 GB of recording in under 0.6 s (KosmicKrisp: 2423 B per 44 B fill),
1148 MB of events in 0.47 s, 1110 MB of MoltenVK command pools in 0.09 s, 12.6 MB per
2^20 timestamps; a submitted pass held up to 179 KB (wired) after the queue went idle, at
any image size, also after a reset, free or destroy: 8 x 16384 clears in one KosmicKrisp
submit took 22 GB. That memory is used again by the process's next command buffers (10
rounds of pool, 2048 submitted passes, free and destroy stayed at 301-317 MB on
KosmicKrisp and 356-363 MB on MoltenVK, also with a pool reset each round and over 10
contexts in turn: fix/out-C; on the mini's M4 a submitted pass held 86 KB on KosmicKrisp
and 36 KB on MoltenVK, and 10 rounds stayed at 150-215 MB: fix/r2/m4), so the charge
comes back at the pool's destroy. KosmicKrisp
kept copies and clears past a reset and a free until the pool's destroy, MoltenVK an
update's data past that. MoltenVK reported a 32768 MB heap (all of the M1 Max), more
than the 22528 MB apps' share, and both drivers a 19169 MB maxBufferSize. One 256 MB
command left 320 MB in the decoder, per context and ring, up to 1 GB each. Each charge
is at least what the M1 probes measured (work/v624-hostmem/kit/costs.py over the probe
output, out/costs.txt). Device memory was charged right already (unchanged).
Upstream: OmacVM policy; nothing posted.
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-venus-commands.c (h-rec-, h-obj- (h-obj-module-size reads
spirv-corpus/pass/dxvk-d3d11-004.spv; h-obj-cache-size gives new caches the driver's
own data of a cache), h-release-, h-heap, h-temp-big,
h-zink-frame-ok; run-venus-limits.py runs h-rec-spare-ok, h-release-end and
h-pass-secondary-ok under libgmalloc too).

